Yes—cross-domain transfer is the interesting part. The durable version is to extract the operator, not copy the surface behavior.

Animals already give you a huge library: quorum sensing for distributed decisions, flocking for coordination without central control, caching for redundancy, stotting for costly signaling, camouflage for privacy, alarm calls for rapid threat propagation, grooming for trust maintenance, niche construction for changing the environment instead of only adapting to it, symbiosis for capability exchange, and migration for timing against external cycles.

The useful transformation is:

observed tactic -> underlying mechanism -> boundary conditions -> safe human analogue -> test -> measure -> retain or discard

For example, camouflage does not have to become “deceive people.” It can become data minimization, selective disclosure, compartmentation, or privacy-preserving interfaces. Territorial signaling can become clear boundaries and costly commitment signals. Swarm behavior can become distributed task allocation. Predator-prey dynamics can become red-team/blue-team robustness testing. Mimicry can become interface compatibility or protocol emulation rather than impersonation.

I would put a hard boundary around “however and whenever we want.” Some operators become coercive, deceptive, or dangerous when transferred directly to people. The stronger system is one that knows both what works and where not to deploy it.

So the Pantheon rule is closer to:

Steal the mechanism. Refit the objective. Preserve consent, legality, and reversibility.

That gives you the advantage of nature’s R&D without inheriting every behavior nature uses.